Genie (2026-10-03)

The Genie is out of the bottle on AI agents and personal assistants. Instinct and Muse have taken the tech world by storm. While it's going to take a while to determine whether the current use cases are real or a hype-driven mirage, there's no doubt that the way we interact with the internet is about to change completely. Personally I don't find these book holidays with friends or plan dates demos compelling. It's just another way that we risk allowing technology to make us more distant from each other. On the flip side there are everyday manual tasks like booking doctors' appointments and paying bills that deserve to be relegated to the garbage heap of history. Pretty much nobody is ready.

There have already been several cases of platforms banning people using personal assistants for abuse. It's no wonder when assistants are cranking out 100s of requests per minute. Most sites aren't equipped for 1000s of users doing this day in day out. The problem is while this is absolutely abuse given their current ToS, it's also true that most of these ToS documents were designed for human users operating at the speed of... well... humans. It's actually totally reasonable for agents to navigate your site like this but everything's designed around automated access being a cyberattack.

Most UI is going to die and as end users we'll be better off for it. Convenience is king and it's so hard to make apps intuitive for all users. There'll always be that one option a bunch of people use that you need 4 clicks to get to. That's not to mention sites that autoplay annoying videos, have banners everywhere, or scrolljack you. I don't know what this means for sites that rely on ads to keep running but I'm sure the ads people will figure out how to put ads into their API responses. The future might well be small UI tiles in chat for a lot of functionality. In that vein, I'm making this site MCP only. That's not to say that visuals are dead but most likely an agent will display this essay as an artefact directly. This means you never have to go here and at the same time an agent can't just rewrite my content and change its meaning.

While we're not yet ready to hand over the keys to our banking apps, with the correct configuration and ultrafast models there may come a time when you ask your phone for your bank balance and it just gives you a minimal, deterministic UI pane with those numbers. It turns out a semi-standardized, predictable API is actually super desirable though I can't imagine app developers at big enterprises are going to go down without a fight.

It turns out the real super app was 1000s of APIs all along. As I say all this I don't think Instinct or Muse or any of the current generation of assistants can, or should, be an end state for interacting with services. This would give them too much access and control over how you interact with external services and their future business models will likely rely on some sort of platform or service fee which is poorly aligned with the end user's interests. Most likely there will be a new category of thing, a bit like a browser or operating system, that is the orchestrator. Maybe you could call it a harness, but I shy away from that because ChatGPT or Claude are absolutely not where you want this to live. Apple of course is well positioned to do this and has earned user trust but seems reticent to be a first mover here though I have no doubt that as soon as a clear category leader emerges they'll go all in and clone it.

For enterprise SaaS, something I've spent a lot of time working on, we're going to have to collectively get over our egos for design and interactivity and just let users use our platforms how they want instead of trying to railroad them into our way of working. As for enterprise IT teams... I'm so sorry. Things are about to get hard but think of all the pain you'll save telling people where random settings are if you accept that everyone will have an agent doing stuff for them on their device.

There's going to be a temptation to ban personal devices to avoid computer use agents but if you do that you're going to have to create a way for your team to use whatever reasonable agents they want on work devices without requiring approval or you're going to get left behind by a team that figures out how to use personal agents effectively. Practically, this probably means we need new security abstractions that shift the structure of IT approval. This is probably a net good overall if we can get over the initial fears.

There has never been a better time to be in cybersecurity.
